# class WithList extends Base { list: number[] = []; n: number; constructor() { super(); this.n = 5 } }
# `n: number;` is defined as undefined and the constructor always stores a number before anybody can read it, so the definition stores 0 and the field stays in its number slot.
# That must not depend on what the other members of the class are: an array literal (can only throw out of memory), a captured variable that lives in an environment,
# a private field below a base class (the initializer tests the brand first), an arrow function that captures `this`, a computed key that is already a property key,
# a call in front of the definition.
# It still depends on them where they can show the field: the arrow function is called before the store, the computed key may be an object below a base class, another object is read below a base class.
# FORM: AsRead
# RUN: valueflow-apply

